" (https://cs.uwaterloo.ca/~cnsun/public/publication/ase11/ase11.pdf)\nciteturn2view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 10; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view0\",\"lineno\":27}); Total lines: 1064\nL0@P0: Towards More Accurate Retrieval of Duplicate Bug\nL1@P0: Reports\nL2@P0: Chengnian Sun∗, David Lo†, Siau-Cheng Khoo∗, Jing Jiang†\nL3@P0: ∗School of Computing, National University of Singapore\nL4@P0: †School of Information Systems, Singapore Management University\nL5@P0: suncn@comp.nus.edu.sg, davidlo@smu.edu.sg, khoosc@comp.nus.edu.sg, jingjiang@smu.edu.sg\nL6@P0: Abstract—In a bug tracking system, different testers or users\nL7@P0: may submit multiple reports on the same bugs, referred to as\nL8@P0: duplicates, which may cost extra maintenance efforts in triaging\nL9@P0: and fixing bugs. In order to identify such duplicates accurately,\nL10@P0: in this paper we propose a retrieval function (REP) to measure\nL11@P0: the similarity between two bug reports. It fully utilizes the\nL12@P0: information available in a bug report including not only the\nL13@P0: similarity of textual content in summary and description fields,\nL14@P0: but also similarity of non-textual fields such as product, com\u0002ponent, version, etc. For more accurate measurement of textual\nL15@P0: similarity, we extend BM25F – an effective similarity formula in\nL16@P0: information retrieval community, specially for duplicate report\nL17@P0: retrieval. Lastly we use a two-round stochastic gradient descent\nL18@P0: to automatically optimize REP for specific bug repositories in a\nL19@P0: supervised learning manner.\nL20@P0: We have validated our technique on three large software\nL21@P0: bug repositories from Mozilla, Eclipse and OpenOffice. The\nL22@P0: experiments show 10–27% relative improvement in recall rate@k\nL23@P0: and 17–23% relative improvement in mean average precision over\nL24@P0: our previous model. We also applied our technique to a very large\nL25@P0: dataset consisting of 209,058 reports from Eclipse, resulting in a\nL26@P0: recall rate@k of 37–71% and mean average precision of 47%.\nL27@P0: I. INTRODUCTION\nL28@P0: Due to complexities of software systems, software bugs\nL29@P0: are prevalent. To manage and keep track of bugs and their\nL30@P0: associated fixes, bug tracking system like Bugzilla1 has been\nL31@P0: proposed and is widely adopted. With such a system, end users\nL32@P0: and testers could report bugs that they encounter. Developers\nL33@P0: could triage, track, and comment on the various bugs that are\nL34@P0: reported.\nL35@P0: Bug reporting however is an uncoordinated distributed pro\u0002cess. End users and testers might report the same defects many\nL36@P0: times in the bug reporting system. This causes an issue as\nL37@P0: different developers should not be assigned the same defect.\nL38@P0: Figuring out which bug reports are duplicate of others is\nL39@P0: typically done manually by a person called the triager. The\nL40@P0: triager would detect if a bug report is a duplicate; if it is,\nL41@P0: the triager would mark this report as a duplicate report and\nL42@P0: the first report as the master report. This process however is\nL43@P0: not scalable for systems with large user base as the process\nL44@P0: could take much time. Consider for example Mozilla; in 2005,\nL45@P0: it was reported that “everyday almost 300 bugs appear that\nL46@P0: need triaging. This is far too much for only the Mozilla\nL47@P0: programmers to handle” [1].\nL48@P0: 1http://www.bugzilla.org/\nL49@P0: To address this issue there have been a number of studies\nL50@P0: that try to partially automate the triaging process. There are\nL51@P0: two general approaches: one is by filtering duplicate reports\nL52@P0: preventing them from reaching the triagers [2], the other is\nL53@P0: by providing a list of top-k related bug reports for each new\nL54@P0: bug report under investigation [3]–[6]. In this study, we focus\nL55@P0: on the second approach for the following reasons. The first\nL56@P0: approach is more difficult and the state-of-the-art approach\nL57@P0: proposed in [2] is only able to remove 8% of the duplicate\nL58@P0: reports. The remaining 92% of the duplicate bug reports would\nL59@P0: still require manual investigation. Furthermore, duplicate bug\nL60@P0: reports are not necessarily bad. Bettenburg et al. notice that\nL61@P0: one bug report might only provide a partial view of the defect,\nL62@P0: while multiple bug reports can complement one another [7].\nL63@P0: Thus, in this study, we focus on providing a technique that\nL64@P0: could help in linking bug reports that are duplicate of one\nL65@P0: another.\nL66@P0: Unfortunately, the accuracy of proposed techniques for\nL67@P0: duplicate bug report detection through ranking [3]–[6] is still\nL68@P0: low. In this work, we show an alternative approach with the\nL69@P0: goal of improving the accuracy of existing techniques. With\nL70@P0: a more accurate technique, triagers could be presented with\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDuplicate Bug Report Detection: How Far Are We? (https://happygirlzt.com/publications/tosem22.pdf)\nciteturn2view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 32; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view2\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 1238\nL0@P0: 97\nL1@P0: Duplicate Bug Report Detection: How Far Are We?\nL2@P0: TING ZHANG, Singapore Management University\nL3@P0: DONGGYUN HAN, Royal Holloway, University of London\nL4@P0: VENKATESH VINAYAKARAO, Chennai Mathematical Institute\nL5@P0: IVANA CLAIRINE IRSAN, BOWEN XU, FERDIAN THUNG, DAVID LO, and LINGXIAO\nL6@P0: JIANG, Singapore Management University\nL7@P0: Many Duplicate Bug Report Detection (DBRD) techniques have been proposed in the research literature. The\nL8@P0: industry uses some other techniques. Unfortunately, there is insufficient comparison among them, and it is\nL9@P0: unclear how far we have been. This work fills this gap by comparing the aforementioned techniques. To\nL10@P0: compare them, we first need a benchmark that can estimate how a tool would perform if applied in a realistic\nL11@P0: setting today. Thus, we first investigated potential biases that affect the fair comparison of the accuracy of\nL12@P0: DBRD techniques. Our experiments suggest that data age and issue tracking system (ITS) choice cause a\nL13@P0: significant difference. Based on these findings, we prepared a new benchmark. We then used it to evaluate\nL14@P0: DBRD techniques to estimate better how far we have been. Surprisingly, a simpler technique outperforms\nL15@P0: recently proposed sophisticated techniques on most projects in our benchmark. In addition, we compared\nL16@P0: the DBRD techniques proposed in research with those used in Mozilla and VSCode. Surprisingly, we observe\nL17@P0: that a simple technique already adopted in practice can achieve comparable results as a recently proposed\nL18@P0: research tool. Our study gives reflections on the current state of DBRD, and we share our insights to benefit\nL19@P0: future DBRD research.\nL20@P0: CCS Concepts: • Software and its engineering → Maintaining software;\nL21@P0: Additional Key Words and Phrases: Bug reports, duplicate bug report detection, deep learning, empirical\nL22@P0: study\nL23@P0: ACM Reference format:\nL24@P0: Ting Zhang, DongGyun Han, Venkatesh Vinayakarao, Ivana Clairine Irsan, Bowen Xu, Ferdian Thung, David\nL25@P0: Lo, and Lingxiao Jiang. 2023. Duplicate Bug Report Detection: How Far Are We?. ACM Trans. Softw. Eng.\nL26@P0: Methodol. 32, 4, Article 97 (May 2023), 32 pages.\nL27@P0: https://doi.org/10.1145/3576042\nL28@P0: This research/project is supported by the National Research Foundation, Singapore, under its Industry Alignment Fund -\nL29@P0: Pre-positioning (IAF-PP) Funding Initiative. Any opinions, findings and conclusions or recommendations expressed in this\nL30@P0: material are those of the author(s) and do not reflect the views of National Research Foundation, Singapore.\nL31@P0: Authors’ addresses: T. Zhang, I. C. Irsan, B. Xu (corresponding author), F. Thung, D. Lo, and L. Jiang, Singapore Man\u0002agement University, Singapore, emails: tingzhang.2019@phdcs.smu.edu.sg, {ivanairsan, bowenxu, ferdianthung, davidlo,\nL32@P0: lxjiang}@smu.edu.sg; D. Han, Royal Holloway, University of London, United Kingdom; email: DongGyun.Han@rhul.ac.uk;\nL33@P0: V. Vinayakarao, Chennai Mathematical Institute, India; email: venkateshv@cmi.ac.in.\nL34@P0: Permission to make digital or hard copies of all or part of this work for personal or classroom use is granted without fee\nL35@P0: provided that copies are not made or distributed for profit or commercial advantage and that copies bear this notice and\nL36@P0: the full citation on the first page. Copyrights for components of this work owned by others than the author(s) must be\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDuplicate Bug Report Detection: How Far Are We? (https://happygirlzt.com/publications/tosem22.pdf)\nciteturn2view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 32; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view2\",\"lineno\":540}); Total lines: 1238\nL522@P12: shows the percentage of BRs which changed their initial states.\nL523@P12: To investigate the impact of ITS bias, we evaluate the three DBRD techniques on BRs (reported\nL524@P12: in 2018–2020) for three sets of projects that use Bugzilla (Eclipse and Mozilla), Jira (Spark and\nL525@P12: Hadoop), and GitHub (Kibana and VSCode) as ITS.\nL526@P12: RQ2: To answer RQ2, other than the three techniques evaluated for answering RQ1, we add two\nL527@P12: more recent techniques, DC-CNN [27] and HINDBR [57]. These two approaches are initially designed\nL528@P12: and also evaluated as a classification task. In our experimental setting, all the BRs submitted\nL529@P12: within a one-year time window are duplicate BRs candidates. For each BR bri,i = 1,...,m,\nL530@P12: where m is the total number of BRs in the test set. We pair it with all its candidate BRs,\nL531@P12: i.e., (bri,bri,1), (bri,bri,2) ... (bri,bri,k ), where k is the total number of candidate BRs of bri ,\nL532@P12: bri,j, j = 1,..., k is a candidate duplicate of bri . The trained classification model is used to predict\nL533@P12: whether each pair is duplicate. The output probability value is then used to rank the possibility\nL534@P12: that each candidate BR be a duplicate one. If two candidate BRs have the same probability value,\nL535@P12: we rank them based on their BR ids in ascending order. We get the top-k recommendations\nL536@P12: 9An example of change history of a BR: https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_activity.cgi?id=122876.\nL537@P12-13: ACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology, Vol. 32, No. 4, Article 97. Pub. date: May 2023.97:14 T. Zhang et al.\nL538@P13: Table 7. The Number of Issues Per Duplicate Recommendation\nL539@P13: Frequency by VSCodeBot\nL540@P13: # predictions 1 2 3 4 5 Total\nL541@P13: # issues 5,016 2,006 1,158 730 2,898 11,808\nL542@P13: and evaluate the performance based on the recommendations. We evaluate the five techniques\nL543@P13: on a new benchmark dataset unaffected by the biases that are found to have a significant and\nL544@P13: substantial impact on DBRD evaluation in RQ1.\nL545@P13: RQ3: To answer RQ3, we investigate the effectiveness of the DBRD techniques used in practice by\nL546@P13: Mozilla and VSCode projects, and compare them with the aforementioned five DBRD techniques.\nL547@P13: Mozilla uses Bugzilla as its ITS, and Bugzilla implements several variants of a DBRD technique\nL548@P13: named FTS.\nL549@P13: 10 We study the source code of Bugzilla to identify how FTS works. Simply put, based on\nL550@P13: the summary input, FTS relies on a BR database and issues SQL queries to search in the database.\nL551@P13: There are two variants of FTS: FULLTEXT_OR and FULLTEXT_AND. For the first variant, the SQL\nL552@P13: queries specify “OR” operations in FTS; otherwise, the SQL queries specify an exact match. We\nL553@P13: inferred the variant of the FTS search used by Mozilla (i.e., the OR variant) by trying to enter new\nL554@P13: BRs into its FTS. We replicated the OR-variant by using the same SQL queries that Bugzilla uses\nL555@P13: on reading the entire summary field.\nL556@P13: Another DBRD technique used in practice is VSCodeBot [7], but its implementation is not pub\u0002licly available, and it only works for the VSCode repository. Thus, we only evaluate the five tech\u0002niques on the test data from the VSCode project. Table 7 presents the number of VSCode issues that\nL557@P13: have potential duplicate recommendations by VSCodeBot made in 2018–2020. Among the 62,092\nL558@P13: VSCode BRs in our dataset, 11,808 BRs got potential duplicate recommendations. VSCodeBot only\nL559@P13: recommends up to five recommendations for an issue.\nL560@P13: 4.2 Evaluation Metrics\nL561@P13: We evaluate the effectiveness of DBRD tools by calculating Recall Rate@k (RR@k). The evaluation\nL562@P13: strategy is consistent with the previous works for the DBRD task [9, 18, 46, 51, 53], i.e., only RR@k\nL563@P13: is used. Note that another metric that has been used in a few DBRD research articles [43, 50] is\nL564@P13: Mean Average Precision (MAP). MAP concerns the position of the ground truth master BR in\nL565@P13: each prediction. A higher MAP means that for each BR in the test set, the model can return the\nL566@P13: ground truth master at a higher place in the ranked result list. The primary reason that prior works,\nL567@P13: as well as our work, do not consider MAP is that it does not simulate the real usage scenario: In real\nL568@P13: use, it is unlikely developers would frequently check the recommendations after 10 BRs. For exam\u0002ple, prior work on understanding practitioners’ expectations of automated fault localization [30]\nL569@P13: has shown that nearly all the respondents (close to 98%) are unwilling to inspect more than ten\nL570@P13: program elements to find the faulty code. Another supporting evidence is for VSCodeBot [7] used\nL571@P13: in the Microsoft Visual Studio Code repository, the largest number of duplicate issue recommen\u0002dations is 5.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDuplicate Bug Report Detection: How Far Are We? (https://happygirlzt.com/publications/tosem22.pdf)\nciteturn2view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 32; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view2\",\"lineno\":680}); Total lines: 1238\nL643@P15-16: ACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology, Vol. 32, No. 4, Article 97. Pub. date: May 2023.Duplicate Bug Report Detection: How Far Are We? 97:17\nL644@P16: Table 8. Mann–Whitney-U with Cliff’s Delta Effect Size |d| on RQ1\nL645@P16: Bias Approach Data p-value |d|\nL646@P16: Age\nL647@P16: REP Eclipse 0.003 0.78 (large)\nL648@P16: Mozilla 0.005 0.72 (large)\nL649@P16: Siamese Pair Eclipse <0.001 1 (large)\nL650@P16: Mozilla 0.003 0.76 (large)\nL651@P16: SABD Eclipse 0.001 0.82 (large)\nL652@P16: Mozilla 0.012 0.66 (large)\nL653@P16: State\nL654@P16: REP Eclipse 0.105 0.44 (medium)\nL655@P16: Mozilla 0.190 0.36 (medium)\nL656@P16: Siamese Pair Eclipse 0.063 0.5 (large)\nL657@P16: Mozilla 0.190 0.36 (medium)\nL658@P16: SABD Eclipse 0.315 0.28 (small)\nL659@P16: Mozilla 0.315 0.28 (small)\nL660@P16: ITS\nL661@P16: REP Jira 0.056 0.36 (medium)\nL662@P16: GitHub <0.001 0.66 (large)\nL663@P16: Siamese Pair Jira <0.001 1 (large)\nL664@P16: GitHub <0.001 0.97 (large)\nL665@P16: SABD Jira <0.001 0.97 (large)\nL666@P16: GitHub <0.001 0.77 (large)\nL667@P16: Table 9. Statistics of Training and Testing Data\nL668@P16: ITS Project Train Test Total\nL669@P16: # BRs (% Dup) # Dup Pairs # BRs (% Dup) # BRs (% Dup)\nL670@P16: Bugzilla Eclipse 19,607 (4.7%) 1,725 7,976 (6.5%) 27,583 (5.2%)\nL671@P16: Mozilla 137,886 (10.1%) 35,474 55,701 (11.2%) 193,587 (10.4%)\nL672@P16: Jira Hadoop 10,276 (2.8%) 328 3,740 (2.5%) 14,016 (2.7%)\nL673@P16: Spark 6,738 (4%) 414 2,841 (3%) 9,579 (3.7%)\nL674@P16: GitHub Kibana 9,849 (2.9%) 376 7,167 (2.6%) 17,016 (2.8%)\nL675@P16: VSCode 40,801 (7.2%) 9,008 21,291 (6.8%) 62,092 (7%)\nL676@P16: 5.2 RQ2. How do the State-of-the-art DBRD Research Tools Perform on Recent Data?\nL677@P16: Based on the findings from RQ1, we evaluate the existing DBRD techniques on a new benchmark\nL678@P16: that omits age bias and ITS bias. Our benchmark contains the recent three-year BRs extracted\nL679@P16: from Bugzilla, Jira, and GitHub. We build this dataset as described in Section 3. Table 9 shows the\nL680@P16: statistics of the training and testing data. Figure 5 shows RR@k from the five approaches on our\nL681@P16: dataset. The x-axis denotes k values from 1 to 10 while the y-axis shows RR@k. Each approach is\nL682@P16: highlighted with a different shape and color. For all datasets, REP outperforms the other approaches\nL683@P16: except for Mozilla and VSCode. On average, REP outperforms SABD by 22.3% in terms of RR@10\nL684@P16: across six project data. As presented in Table 9, both Mozilla and VSCode have the largest number\nL685@P16: of BRs and duplicates BRs. For these two projects, SABD shows comparable results with REP and\nL686@P16: even outperforms on VSCode dataset by 9% in terms of RR@10. Siamese Pair, HINDBR, and DC-CNN\nL687@P16: show fluctuating results. Siamese Pair presents higher RR@k values on Eclipse, Mozilla, and\nL688@P16: VSCode, while it demonstrates lower RR@k than HINDBR and Hadoop on Spark and Kibana.\nL689@P16: Component Analysis. As shown in Figure 5, REP is the winner in five out of the six datasets\nL690@P16: and it takes advantage of the information from most of the fields. Thus, we investigate REP to\nL691@P16: understand the contribution of each component in DBRD. REP initializes the weights of the textual\nL692@P16-17: ACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology, Vol. 32, No. 4, Article 97. Pub. date: May 2023.97:18 T. Zhang et al.\nL693@P17: Fig. 5. Recall Rate@k in the test data of Eclipse, Mozilla, Hadoop, Spark, Kibana, and VSCode.\nL694@P17: features higher than the rest of the features. Additionally, the initial weight of the summary is also\nL695@P17: higher than the weight of the description. Besides, among the total 19 parameters tuned by gradient\nL696@P17: descent, 14 parameters relate to the textual fields. Based on the observation, our assumption is that\nL697@P17: textual fields are the most crucial components among all the fields considered. To further verify our\nL698@P17-18: ACM Transactions on Software Engineering and Methodology, Vol. 32, No. 4, Article 97. Pub. date: May 2023.Duplicate Bug Report Detection: How Far Are We? 97:19\nL699@P18: Table 10. Investigation of which Component Benefits REP\nL700@P18: RR@k All w/o\nL701@P18: description short_desc product component priority\nL702@P18: 1 0.460 0.327 0.350 0.456 0.458 0.450\nL703@P18: 2 0.544 0.415 0.458 0.527 0.554 0.540\nL704@P18: 3 0.610 0.456 0.494 0.575 0.598 0.602\nL705@P18: 4 0.646 0.490 0.510 0.617 0.633 0.637\nL706@P18: 5 0.673 0.515 0.533 0.644 0.658 0.665\nL707@P18: 6 0.690 0.531 0.552 0.662 0.663 0.679\nL708@P18: 7 0.704 0.544 0.569 0.671 0.671 0.694\nL709@P18: 8 0.706 0.556 0.579 0.681 0.683 0.706\nL710@P18: 9 0.715 0.565 0.600 0.687 0.683 0.712\nL711@P18: 10 0.721 0.573 0.613 0.698 0.692 0.717\nL712@P18: hypothesis, we investigate the contributions of each field value in REP. We run REP on the Eclipse\nL713@P18: dataset and each time set a certain field value as empty. Table 10 demonstrates the performances\nL714@P18: when we set each field as empty. We can find that REP performs the best when all the information\nL715@P18: is present. When the values of the fields of severity, priority, product, and component are left\nL716@P18: empty, the performance is similar to the result when all the information is considered. The RR@k\nL717@P18: is decreased at most 4%. However, when textual information is absent, we can observe that the\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNoul - TypeSafe AI (https://docs.typesafe.ai/primitives/noul)\nciteturn2view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"lineno\":175}); Total lines: 280\nL145: The two-question request above gives the code enough to route the message. The example below escalates to a person when the customer asks for one, and raises the priority when they have been in touch before. A value in the middle on either question goes to a reviewer instead of a code path:\nL146: \nL147:     `from typesafe_sdk import Noul, NoulCriteria, TypeSafeClient\nL148: \nL149:     SUPPORT_QUESTIONS = {\nL150:         \"is_human_escalation\": Noul(\nL151:             instructions=\"Is the customer asking for a human agent?\",\nL152:         ),\nL153:         \"is_repeat_contact\": Noul(\nL154:             instructions=\"Has the customer contacted support about this before?\",\nL155:             criteria=NoulCriteria(\nL156:                 true=\"Mentions a prior attempt, ticket, or that they have asked before\",\nL157:                 false=\"No sign of any previous contact\",\nL158:             ),\nL159:         ),\nL160:     }\nL161: \nL162:     YES = 0.8\nL163:     NO = 0.2\nL164: \nL165: \nL166:     def route(message: str) -> None:\nL167:         with TypeSafeClient() as client:\nL168:             response = client.system_one(\nL169:                 model=\"jev-latest\",\nL170:                 state=message,\nL171:                 questions=SUPPORT_QUESTIONS,\nL172:             )\nL173:         answers = response.answers\nL174: \nL175:         wants_human = answers[\"is_human_escalation\"].noul\nL176:         repeat = answers[\"is_repeat_contact\"].noul\nL177: \nL178:         if NO < wants_human < YES or NO < repeat < YES:\nL179:             # The model isn't sure either way. Let a person decide.\nL180:             send_to_review(message)\nL181:             return\nL182:         priority = \"high\" if repeat > YES else \"normal\"\nL183:         if wants_human > YES:\nL184:             route_to_agent(message, priority=priority)\nL185:         else:\nL186:             route_to_bot(message, priority=priority)\nL187:     `\nL188: For the message above, the noul answer value for `is_human_escalation` is 0.99 and `is_repeat_contact` is 0.93, so the code routes it to an agent at high priority. The message “How do I reset my password?” is 0.07 on both questions and is routed to the bot. The thresholds live in your code. If reviewers see too many messages, narrow the gap between `NO` and `YES`. If too many wrong routes get through, widen it.\nL189: If you later need to know whether the message mentions a payment, or whether it contains personal data, add another Noul to `SUPPORT_QUESTIONS`. The request count stays at one.\nL190: ##\nL191: \nL192: cite13†​ L193: \nL194: Structured instructions\nL195: Instructions can be an object instead of a string, with the question in one field and supplementary data in the others. cite12†Use structure in the questions covers when that helps. Here it’s used for a question built using code: a resume that has just arrived is compared against records in a candidate database that might be the same person. Each record goes into a `potential_duplicate` field as it is, the `question` is the same for every record, and all the records are checked in one request.\nL196: The code-generated question keys contain each record’s database ID: The response:\nL197: \nL198:     `{\nL199:       \"model\": \"jev-1.13.0\",\nL200:       \"answers\": {\nL201:         \"same_as_record_18\": {\nL202:           \"type\": \"noul\",\nL203:           \"noul\": 0.74\nL204:         },\nL205:         \"same_as_record_42\": {\nL206:           \"type\": \"noul\",\nL207:           \"noul\": 0.09\nL208:         },\nL209:         \"same_as_record_77\": {\nL210:           \"type\": \"noul\",\nL211:           \"noul\": 0.08\nL212:         }\nL213:       },\nL214:       \"usage\": {\nL215:         \"input_tokens\": 535,\nL216:         \"output_tokens\": 58\nL217:       }\nL218:     }\nL219:     `\nL220: Each answer is the probability that the resume is for the person in that record. Record 18 spells the name differently but matches on location and employer, and gets 0.74. Record 42 has the same name in a different city with a different employer, and gets 0.09. Record 77 is a similar name at the same location with a different employer, and gets 0.08. Threshold each value in your code, as in cite20†Handling multiple Noul answers in code , and send the middle values to a person.\nL221: With the Python SDK, the questions are built from the candidate records. The question text is fixed and the record changes:\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://docs.typesafe.ai/llms.txt)\nciteturn2view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/plain; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view4\",\"lineno\":1}); Total lines: 116\nL0: # TypeSafe AI\nL1: \nL2: > How to use TypeSafe's System One API\nL3: - [Introduction](https://docs.typesafe.ai/introduction.md): Jev is TypeSafe's flagship model and the first System One model. Send state and typed questions; get structured answers your code can use directly.\nL4: - [Quick start](https://docs.typesafe.ai/introduction/quickstart.md): Prefer to just dive in? Here's everything you need to get started immediately.\nL5: - [Jev with coding agents](https://docs.typesafe.ai/introduction/coding-agents.md): What Jev is (and isn't) when you're using a coding agent.\nL6: - [Example use cases](https://docs.typesafe.ai/concepts/use-case-map.md): Explore TypeSafe use cases by industry and turn promising ideas into software workflows.\nL7: - [System One](https://docs.typesafe.ai/concepts/system-one.md): System One models make fast, structured decisions for software. Jev is TypeSafe's flagship model and the first System One model.\nL8: - [State](https://docs.typesafe.ai/concepts/state.md): What state is, how to structure it, and how to give a System One model the context it needs.\nL9: - [Primitives (Questions)](https://docs.typesafe.ai/primitives.md): The three TypeSafe question types (Choice, Score, Noul), the typed answers they return, how to choose between them, and how to ask several at once.\nL10: - [Choice](https://docs.typesafe.ai/primitives/choice.md): A Choice is a System One question type for selecting one option from a defined set. The answer includes the selected option, a probability for each option, and confidence.\nL11: - [Score](https://docs.typesafe.ai/primitives/score.md): A Score is a System One question type for rating content against ordered, descriptive levels. The answer includes a score, a probability for each level, and confidence.\nL12: - [Noul](https://docs.typesafe.ai/primitives/noul.md): A Noul question asks the TypeSafe model to evaluate a yes/no question and return the probability that the answer is yes.\nL13: - [Advanced: structure](https://docs.typesafe.ai/primitives/advanced.md): Instructions, Choice options, Score levels, and Noul criteria all accept JSON structure.\nL14: - [Confidence](https://docs.typesafe.ai/confidence.md): How TypeSafe reports certainty, how it differs from probability, and how to use it to control system behavior.\nL15: - [How to build with TypeSafe](https://docs.typesafe.ai/concepts/how-to-build-with-system-one.md): Design AI-powered software by keeping code in control and giving System One narrow, structured decisions.\nL16: - [AI primer](https://docs.typesafe.ai/introduction/machine-learning-primer.md): Why TypeSafe trains decision models with calibrated probabilities instead of optimizing for generated text.\nL17: - [Patterns](https://docs.typesafe.ai/patterns.md): Architectural patterns for building systems with TypeSafe.\nL18: - [Speculative fan-out](https://docs.typesafe.ai/patterns/fan-out.md): Send many questions in a single call, including speculative ones, and let your code decide what's relevant.\nL19: - [Confidence-gated routing](https://docs.typesafe.ai/patterns/confidence-routing.md): Use confidence as a second axis. The answer tells you what; confidence tells you whether to act.\n"
